  "title": "Assessment Types Overview",
  "content": "<b>Assessment types describe the viewpoint, level of interaction, and technical layer used to answer a security question.</b> They are dimensions that can be combined, not competing labels. An engagement may be external, active, and application-level at the same time.\n\n<b>External and internal</b> describe viewpoint. An external assessment starts from outside the trusted network and reveals the public attack surface. An internal assessment begins from an approved position inside the environment and can reveal private services, trust relationships, and controls that an outsider cannot see directly. Neither viewpoint is complete by itself.\n\n<b>Passive and active</b> describe interaction. Passive work reviews existing evidence or observes traffic without deliberately stimulating the target. Active testing sends requests, authenticates, scans, or otherwise interacts with systems. Active does not mean unsafe, but its intensity must match authorization and operational risk. Fragile production services may require passive review first, carefully limited checks, owner supervision, or a separate maintenance window.\n\n<b>Host-based</b> assessment examines an endpoint or server from the inside: installed software, patches, local permissions, services, accounts, policies, logs, and scheduled tasks. It answers what is present and how the individual system is configured.\n\n<b>Network-based</b> assessment examines reachability and communication between systems: exposed ports, protocols, filtering, segmentation, and trust paths. It answers what can connect and whether that access is appropriate.\n\n<b>Application-level</b> assessment examines how software handles input, identity, sessions, authorization, data, and business functions. Application findings often require code or framework changes rather than only firewall or host changes.\n\nChoose the combination that matches the question, scope, and tolerance for disruption. Record the viewpoint and method with each result, because a finding that is absent from one assessment type may still be visible from another. Route results to the owners who can act: endpoint teams for host configuration, network teams for reachability, and application teams for software behavior.",
  "links": [
    "internal and external views",
